<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Red River Resistance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Red River Rebellion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Red River Resistance was the rising of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-colony">Red River Settlement</a> in 1869–1870 against the transfer of Rupert's Land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> without their consent. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a>, they set up a provisional government and negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba-act">Manitoba Act</a> of 1870, which created the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a>, before a military expedition arrived and Riel fled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-river-resistance.jpg" alt="Red River Resistance" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, believed to be Joseph Langevin.[3][4] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ProvisionalMetisGovernment.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
